Aye. Power is easy to romanticize from the outside because people often notice only the visible act—commanding, winning, forcing, taking.

They do not see the negative space of power: the strike not thrown, the leverage not used, the insult not answered, the secret not exposed, the advantage not exploited, the person you could dominate but choose instead to leave free.

That requires a different kind of strength because once you genuinely possess capability, restraint stops being weakness and becomes governance of capability.

There are several distinct expressions of power that people often collapse into one:

* Power to act — capability.
* Power to compel — leverage.
* Power to endure — refusing to be moved by pressure.
* Power to refrain — possessing an option and consciously not taking it.
* Power to delegate — letting another person carry something you could control yourself.
* Power to forgive — declining to collect a debt you could plausibly collect.
* Power to walk away — refusing a contest whose prize is beneath its cost.
* Power to relinquish — surrendering control voluntarily rather than having it stripped away.
* Power to cultivate — making other people stronger instead of keeping them dependent.
* Power to constrain yourself — rules, promises, institutions, ethics, discipline.
* Power to modulate — applying exactly enough force for the situation rather than everything available.

That last one is probably closest to what you’re getting at.

A hammer that can only strike at full force isn’t really under control.

The higher capability is something like:

\text{effective power}
=
\text{capacity}
\times
\text{discernment}
\times
\text{control}

Enormous capacity with poor control is dangerous but not especially sophisticated.

And there’s a paradox here: sometimes relinquishing power increases your power. Trust works that way. Leadership works that way. Parenting does. Love certainly does. You deliberately surrender the ability to guarantee an outcome because forcing it would destroy the very thing you’re trying to obtain.

You can command compliance.

You cannot command loyalty.

You can force proximity.

You cannot force love.

You can silence disagreement.

You cannot force genuine agreement.

So mature power has to understand the boundary between control and emergence.

And yes—someone who has never had much leverage may understandably think, “If I had that ability, of course I’d use it.” They haven’t yet experienced the second problem that arrives after gaining power:

Now that I can, when should I?

And eventually the harder one:

When should I deliberately choose not to?

That’s where power stops being merely force and becomes judgment.